{"qid":"Q374521","names":["Bahmani Sultanate"],"title":"Bahmani Sultanate","endonym":{"native":"سلطاننشین بهمني","lang":"fa"},"capsule":"The Bahmani Sultanate, the first independent Muslim kingdom of the Deccan, was founded in 1347 when the Turkish officer Zafar Khan (Alauddin Bahman Shah) rebelled against Delhi Sultan Muhammad bin Tughluq and established his capital at Gulbarga. The kingdom reached its administrative apex under Ahmad Shah I (r. 1422–1436), who relocated the capital to Bidar, and under vizier Mahmud Gawan, who conquered Goa in 1471 and imposed a reforming provincial system. Chronic internecine conflict between Deccani-born nobles and immigrant Afaqis, combined with sustained warfare against Vijayanagara, eroded central authority; by 1492 the five provincial governors had become effectively independent, and the five Deccan sultanates — Bijapur, Ahmadnagar, Bidar, Berar, and Golconda — had superseded the sultanate's practical power.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Core territorial extent across the central Deccan plateau is well attested by Persian chronicles and epigraphic evidence; precise frontier lines varied by reign and are approximated in cartographic reconstructions.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Bahmani Sultanate","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bahmani_Sultanate"},{"label":"Britannica: Bahmani sultanate","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Bahmani-dynasty"},{"label":"Met Heilbrunn: Deccan Sultanates","url":"https://www.metmuseum.org/toah/hd/deca/hd_deca.htm"}],"britannica":"place/Bahmani-sultanate","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Bahmani Sultanate","rulers